    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is collagen a complete protein?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. Collagen contains no tryptophan, one of the nine essential amino acids, and is low in leucine and several others. That gives it a protein quality score of zero on the PDCAAS scale used by regulators. Collagen grams still count as protein, but they should supplement complete proteins like meat, eggs, dairy, soy or a good plant blend rather than replace them."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is collagen or whey better for building muscle?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Whey, clearly. Trials comparing them find whey stimulates more muscle protein synthesis, and a 10-week training study found whey produced greater muscle thickness gains even when extra leucine was added to the collagen. Collagen may help connective tissue, but for muscle size and preservation, a complete, leucine-rich protein such as whey is the better choice per gram."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much collagen should I take per day?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most research uses about 2.5 to 15 g a day, depending on the goal. Skin studies often use 2.5 to 10 g, joint studies around 10 g, and one connective tissue study used about 15 g of gelatin with vitamin C before exercise. A daily 10 to 15 g serving covers most studied doses. This is general information, not medical advice."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I mix collagen into hot coffee?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Hydrolyzed collagen peptides are designed to dissolve in hot and cold liquids, and unflavored versions add little taste, which is why coffee is the most popular way to take them. Heat from coffee does not meaningfully damage collagen peptides for supplement purposes. Some buyers find certain brands mix less easily in cold drinks, where a frother helps."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does collagen protein count toward my daily protein?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Partly. The grams are real protein, but collagen is incomplete, so it cannot replace complete protein one for one. Industry-funded research suggests up to about a third of daily protein can come from collagen without creating an essential amino acid shortfall. Count it toward your total, but get most of your target from complete sources."
          }
        },
