        <div id="what-is-collagen" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Collagen Protein Powder Actually Is
          </h2>

          <p>
            Collagen is the most abundant protein in the body. It forms the structural scaffolding of skin, tendons, ligaments, cartilage and bone, and it is built from long, tightly wound chains rich in three amino acids: glycine, proline and hydroxyproline. Collagen powders are made from the same material in animals, usually cattle hides, fish skin and scales, or chicken cartilage, which manufacturers boil, purify and break into smaller pieces.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most powders sold as collagen peptides or hydrolyzed collagen have been treated with enzymes that cut the long collagen chains into short fragments. That is what makes them dissolve in hot or cold liquid with almost no taste or texture, and it is the main practical difference from gelatin, which is partially broken collagen that still gels when it cools. Nutritionally, collagen peptides, gelatin and bone broth protein are close relatives: all three are overwhelmingly collagen.
          </p>

          <p>
            Labels typically list grams of collagen and grams of protein separately, and the protein figure is usually a little lower. Vital Proteins, Great Lakes Wellness and Garden of Life all list 20 g of collagen as about 18 g of protein. That gap reflects the way protein is calculated from nitrogen content, not missing product. What the protein number does not tell you is that this protein is incomplete, which is the most important fact on this page.
          </p>

          <p>
            Collagen powder is not a whey replacement and was never designed to be one. It is a specialized supplement, bought mainly for skin, hair, nails and joints, that happens to carry a protein number on the label. If you are shopping for a shake to build muscle, our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">best whey protein isolate</a> guide is the better starting point, and our <a href="/best-casein-protein-powder/">best casein protein powder</a> guide covers the slow-digesting option.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="collagen-vs-whey" class="scroll-mt-24">
