            2. Collagen vs Whey: The Honest Comparison
          </h2>

          <p>
            The comparison buyers search for most is collagen versus whey, and the answer depends on what you want. For muscle, whey wins clearly. For a tasteless protein that dissolves in hot coffee, collagen wins. For skin and joints, collagen has a body of research, much of it industry-funded, that whey does not. They do different jobs, and plenty of people reasonably use both.
          </p>

          <p>
            The core difference is amino acid composition. Whey is a complete protein with all nine essential amino acids and roughly 10 percent leucine, the amino acid that acts as the main on switch for muscle protein synthesis. Collagen has no tryptophan at all, very little leucine, and low levels of several other essential amino acids. Per 20 g, whey supplies roughly 2 g of leucine, while collagen supplies roughly 0.5 to 0.7 g.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
