            Dose came first. Most clinical collagen research uses roughly 2.5 to 15 g a day, depending on the outcome studied, and many brands build a serving around 10 to 20 g. A full 20 g serving is convenient if you want one dose a day; a smaller 10 or 11 g scoop gives more flexibility. We did not penalize smaller scoops as long as the label was clear about them, but we did note where you need two scoops to reach a typical dose.
          </p>

          <p>
            Certification was the tiebreaker. Collagen is a supplement category with plenty of marketing and relatively few independent certifications, so a product with NSF Certified for Sport status, such as Thorne Collagen Fit, earns a clear edge for athletes who are drug-tested. Diet certifications like Keto Certified, Certified Paleo Friendly and NSF Gluten Free are useful for specific buyers but do not test for banned substances or contaminants in the same way.
          </p>

          <p>
            Simplicity mattered too. The best collagen products for most people have one ingredient and no flavor, because collagen's main appeal is that it disappears into whatever you are already drinking. Add-ins such as probiotics, nicotinamide riboside, hyaluronic acid or vitamin C are not harmful, but they raise the price and the evidence for most of them in this format is thin. We flagged them rather than rewarding them.
          </p>
