        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is collagen a complete protein?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Collagen contains no tryptophan, one of the nine essential amino acids, and is low in leucine and several others. That gives it a protein quality score of zero on the PDCAAS scale used by regulators. Collagen grams still count as protein, but they should supplement complete proteins like meat, eggs, dairy, soy or a good plant blend rather than replace them.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is collagen or whey better for building muscle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Whey, clearly. Trials comparing them find whey stimulates more muscle protein synthesis, and a 10-week training study found whey produced greater muscle thickness gains even when extra leucine was added to the collagen. Collagen may help connective tissue, but for muscle size and preservation, a complete, leucine-rich protein such as whey is the better choice per gram.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much collagen should I take per day?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most research uses about 2.5 to 15 g a day, depending on the goal. Skin studies often use 2.5 to 10 g, joint studies around 10 g, and one connective tissue study used about 15 g of gelatin with vitamin C before exercise. A daily 10 to 15 g serving covers most studied doses. This is general information, not medical advice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I mix collagen into hot coffee?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Hydrolyzed collagen peptides are designed to dissolve in hot and cold liquids, and unflavored versions add little taste, which is why coffee is the most popular way to take them. Heat from coffee does not meaningfully damage collagen peptides for supplement purposes. Some buyers find certain brands mix less easily in cold drinks, where a frother helps.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does collagen protein count toward my daily protein?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Partly. The grams are real protein, but collagen is incomplete, so it cannot replace complete protein one for one. Industry-funded research suggests up to about a third of daily protein can come from collagen without creating an essential amino acid shortfall. Count it toward your total, but get most of your target from complete sources.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is marine collagen better than bovine collagen?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not meaningfully for most people. Marine collagen is mostly type I and bovine is mostly types I and III, but both are digested into similar amino acids and peptides. Marine collagen suits pescatarians and people who avoid beef, while bovine is usually cheaper. Anyone with a fish allergy should avoid marine collagen and multi-collagen blends that include fish.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which collagen is best for athletes who are drug tested?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Choose a product with NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport certification and verify the exact product in the certifier's online database. Thorne Collagen Fit carries NSF Certified for Sport status, and Momentous sells a certified collagen as well. Diet certifications such as Keto Certified or NSF Gluten Free do not test for banned substances.
              </p>
